00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.360
It's got puppets but it also has animation in a lot of different styles.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.240
And then, if you wait long enough, it also has some live-action sequences.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.840
All of these different styles are one of
the things that makes it really hard to interpret.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.300
These videos aren't interested
in playing by anybody else's rules.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.200
And that makes it really exciting
to watch.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:07.330
But last, and probably most importantly,
is the amazing attention to detail these videos have.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:11.350
The number of callbacks and
easter eggs in every episode is astounding!

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.230
Some of them, you have to practically watch frame by frame to even see!

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.830
And you know that's the kind of stuff that makes me,

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.290
and obviously tons of other Theorists out there,

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.800
really excited! There are a lot
of different ways to interpret 'Don't Hug Me,'

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.140
But today for our first theory on
this series - That's right; I said, "First."

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.239
More on that later. - we're going to talk
about what the whole thing means.

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:35.269
An overview of the main themes of the
whole series.

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:38.840
Granted, we're not the first people to
try and do this. YouTube and Reddit were

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.440
flooded with theories when the last
episode went up, but I really tried to do

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.650
my homework on this one, and I got an
explanation for 'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared,'

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.069
that will convince even the most
skeptical of theorists. Be warned,

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:51.079
comprehensive Spoilers lie ahead,

00:02:51.079 --> 00:02:55.099
but then again this entire thing is on
YouTube, and it's just 6 episodes that

00:02:55.099 --> 00:02:58.579
are between 3 and 8 minutes each.
So just go watch it. Like right now.

00:02:58.579 --> 00:02:59.390
Anyway,

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.380
Y'all ready? Faaantastic! Let's get, creative!

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.360
If we're hoping to explain what's going on in 'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared' we first

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.130
need to establish what kind of world
we're in. A lot of theories look at

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.820
bizarre events in this series as
evidence of something sinister such as

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.180
the three main characters to whom I'll
be referring to from here on out is

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.100
The Red Guy, The Yellow Guy, and the Duck, being stuck
in purgatory or some sort of nightmare.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.599
To that I say,

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:28.160
Occam's razor, my friend. A better
explanation is one that's a lot simpler...

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.970
These three are just stars of a
children's television show.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.569
Granted, it may not be a very nice
children's television show, but the

00:03:35.569 --> 00:03:36.590
evidence is there.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.739
There are several instances in which we
see the inner workings of a television

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:40.400
show.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.730
We see clapboards and cameras right in
episode one, at the start of the

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.290
creativity montage.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:48.940
Then again in episode 4, when The Red Guy follows the computer wire to the next

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.780
room, and in episode 6, as The Lamp
explains dreaming to The Yellow Guy.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.840
You can also tell that most of the time
these characters are on sets. This is

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:03.709
most obvious when Duck revolts in episode 5, saying "Enough, I don't want to do this anymore!"

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.909
The perspective of the video changes. Indicating that Duck has hit the camera that's filming him, and

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:10.970
with the camera looks upward, we can see
the characters aren't actually in a

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.200
house with a ceiling, but in a studio
with lights overhead. The other obvious

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.380
tip off, is that our three main
characters are basically archetypes of

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.669
what you see on Sesame Street. The Yellow Guy is a human puppet like Burt or Ernie,

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:26.509
The Duck is an animal puppet like
Snuffleupagus or Big Bird, and The Red

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:29.870
Guy is some sort of humanoid monster, like Grover or

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:34.039
Oscar The Grouch.  Or those aliens that go "MEEPEPEPEPEPEPE,

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:38.750
uh-huhm, uh-huh" all the time. Those things utterly freaked me out by the way. And if

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.080
this is indeed a TV show it would
explain some of the easter eggs we keep

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.860
seeing in later episodes. As 'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared' progresses, we start to see

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.430
some of the same props appearing over
and over again. At the beginning of

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.360
episode 4 we can see a statue of Malcolm,
from the previous episode, on the fire

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:56.870
place next to the clock. At the start of
episode 5 we see the number chart

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:57.900
that the computer uses

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.990
in episode 4 attached to the
refrigerator, and then there are other

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.139
props like the red, blue and yellow
baseball cap that keeps showing up

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:05.490
episode after episode.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:08.620
These aren't just cool Easter eggs they're
there for a reason. To decorate the set

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.300
with props they've used before, instead
of having to buy entirely new props and

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.530
set pieces every time. This is common not just within TV shows, but also across

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.539
productions as a way to save money.

00:05:18.539 --> 00:05:23.220
There are examples of this practice all
OVER film and TV, like the newspaper from

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.750
'No Country For Old Men' showing up in the
'Texas Chainsaw Massacre' and even better

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:31.760
'Modern Family'.  The armor from StarShip Troopers also makes an appearance in

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.780
'FireFly', or 'Star-Trek' literally reusing
an entire space station in two

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.940
completely separate productions!

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.020
There's one other juicy bit of Theorist Bait in episode 6, that strongly points

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.260
in the whole thing being a TV show as
well. When The Yellow Guy is in bed, looking

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.400
through a scrapbook,

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.060
we see a picture of The Red Guy, who
wasn't in episode 5 except for the credits.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:59.840
And who's bed is empty at the start of episode 6, wearing a graduation cap. Other theorists have suggested that
this means that Red Guy is a teenager,

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.139
who grew up and went out to the real world, but going off to college is also

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:08.550
a way for a television show to explain the
sudden absence of a main character. For

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:13.380
instance, at the end of season 4 of 'Blue's
Clues,' the original host Steve announces

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.610
that he's leaving Blue to go off to
college, when in actuality that was just

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:21.840
the show transitioning to a new host
letting Steve go and do whatever weird

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.580
artsy films that he's working on now
and NO, internet, he did not go off and

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.990
die of a heroin overdose he just did a
TV show like three years ago.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.990
Fact check internet. MAN,

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.320
you guys are dark! Then again we are
talking about a series of videos where

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.280
one of the puppets dies in a vat of oil
and another gets his intestines ripped

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.290
out. So...yeah.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.980
Internet is dark dark place. Alright,
great! So 'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared' is a

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:50.660
kids TV show. Awesome, but what does it mean? What the heck are the makers of the

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.370
show trying to say?

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:56.120
Well the first part of this is fairly
obvious: television messages that are

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.750
delivered to children are corrupting
them as much as they're helping them.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.000
Those episodes of 'Sesame Street'? Oh-ho yeah!

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.080
Leading your kids down a dark path of
brainwashing destruction!

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.860
I'm looking at you, Big Bird! At least
according to the DHMIS. You don't think so?

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.280
Just look at each episode! The pattern
here is pretty clear: each one has a

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.630
theme you expected the other show like
'Sesame Street': creativity, how time works,

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.790
love, technology and computers, 
 healthy eating, and dreams. But in every instance

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:27.320
that message gets corrupted and not just
by decaying flesh or catching your dad

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.020
watching porn or a human heart covered in
glitter.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:33.950
Let's take it episode by episode. Episode
1 is all about the virtues of creativity,

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:38.480
except it portrays certain types of
creativity as being right and wrong,

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:41.690
claiming that green isn't a creative
color and that The Yellow Guy's picture

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:45.650
of a clown deserves to be destroyed
because it's somehow creative in the

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:46.730
wrong way.

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:51.230
Episode 2 tries to explain the concept
of time, but that explanation is overly

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.920
simplistic and teaches that time is
important

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.850
even though kids still can't understand
it from the way it's being taught. The clock

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:02.710
answers questions about time like "But when
did it start?" "And when will it stop?" with weird

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.990
evasive answers like "Time is simple

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:09.640
and I am a clock." Then when The Duck finally
tries to explain time scientifically, The

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:10.150
Clock

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:15.250
yells until Yellow Guy's ears literally
start to bleed! In episode 3 the Yellow

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:21.790
Guy learns about love from a butterfly--
"Pesky bee!" or bee-- "A little baby pigeon." or

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:26.710
little baby pigeon or...whatever, and
teaches him about feelings. But then

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:30.490
later learns that love is only ok when
it's followed in a certain way,

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:33.220
particularly as it applies to religion.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:36.910
Episode 4 is all about computers and how
they can teach us a lot about the world,

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:40.540
but at the end simply conclude that in
the digital world people end up doing

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:44.050
frivolous things like fashion and
shopping and mindless entertainment.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.830
They're literally getting sucked into a
computer and distracted with all these

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.310
superficial things rather than the
information they were looking for in the

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.490
first place. Then in episode 5 the
characters are supposed to learn about

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.070
how to eat healthfully, but the advice of
the can

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:01.960
meat is confusing and contradictory. And
this food pyramid is just

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:07.480
terrifying! "The food groups can easily be
sorted using the simple health shape." It's

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.840
also about how certain name-brand
products are good for you and others are

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:11.470
bad.

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.610
The underlying theme here is clear: the
kids show these three characters are in

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:19.420
throughout the entirety of 'Don't Hug Me,
I'm Scared' sets out to deliver lessons

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:24.010
about the world, but either fails
miserably or succeeds and intentionally

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:29.020
brainwashing kids to a specific point of
view. But why? Why are the creators of the

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.570
show so cynical about kids programming?

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:35.770
Are they trying to say that there's some
big conspiracy behind kids TV shows? Well,

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:37.780
yeah, actually.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.810
Definitely seems that way. Dig a little
bit deeper and there's evidence that the

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:45.550
show's creators are railing against not
just kids TV but the makers of kids TV

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:50.380
and what their goals really are. Not
educating kids but in making money.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:54.610
Throughout the series we see visuals all
about earning money, such as the very

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:59.980
first thing you see in 'Don't Hug Me,' a
newspaper which reads "Stocks Flying High,"

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:03.610
along with a figure that seems to imply
that the value of the British pound is

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:07.000
increasing. We also have subtler clues
like a board game in the background of

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.870
episode 4 that's titled "Money Win." But
the most important element of this

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.470
very small detail. Any fan of 'Don't Hug
Me, I'm Scared' knows that each of the

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:20.330
episodes takes place on June 19, but what
you have to look closer to see is the

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:24.350
year. You can actually figure it out from
episode 1. That newspaper headline

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:27.560
about stocks soaring in the UK is no
accident,

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:31.820
and it's not fiction. If you look at
stock records for June 19 over the last

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:35.660
hundred years, there's one year in
particular where stock markets were

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:42.830
hitting record highs on that date: in
1955. In 1955, stock markets all over the

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:47.779
world, including the US and UK, broke
practically every record imaginable when

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:51.980
the market's open. And if it was good
evidence in episode 1, in episode 2 we

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:56.060
get our definitive proof. When The Clock
mentions that the past is far behind us,

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:59.420
we see a picture of all three main
characters with the date underneath that

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:05.990
reads 19-06-55. The 19-06 part obviously
stands for Jun 19,

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:10.400
since the Brits put their day before
the month, with the 55 confirming our

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.510
year. But why? Why 1955?

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:19.310
Well 1955 was actually a huge deal in
British television history. Prior to

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:23.420
1954, the British
Broadcasting Corporation, or BBC, held a

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:28.130
monopoly on British television, but it
was 1954 that the UK passed

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:32.600
what became known as the Television Act,
in an effort to bring an end to that BBC

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:36.440
monopoly. This act led to the
establishment of the first alternative

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:41.480
network called ITV, for Independent TV in
1954.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:45.290
Where the BBC is sponsored by the British
government, ITV is

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:49.670
independent, and therefore has to make its
money through advertising. Now in

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:52.190
interpreting the series other YouTube
channels,

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:56.570
most notably YouTube Explained, have covered
this topic, but they've said in the

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:59.180
context of 'Don't Hug Me' that this was a
good

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.290
evolution. I see it the opposite way, and
I'm pretty sure the creators of 'Don't

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:04.940
Hug Me, I'm Scared' would agree,

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:10.759
and here's why: this was THE. FIRST. TIME
that advertising appeared in British

00:12:10.759 --> 00:12:14.959
television, marking an inflection point
at which television went from a medium

00:12:14.959 --> 00:12:19.339
to communicate and entertain to a
medium to make a profit, thereby giving

00:12:19.339 --> 00:12:25.490
executives and advertisers as much and
often times more power than the creators.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:28.759
and Britain was especially sensitive to
TV advertising.

00:12:28.759 --> 00:12:32.630
I mean in the US we were already used
to interrupting TV program with ads,

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:36.949
or not even interrupting them at all. In
the US, it was actually standard

00:12:36.949 --> 00:12:40.670
practice at the time to believe those
regular shows into an ad

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:43.399
so one minute you be watching The
Flintstones and then all of a sudden

00:12:43.399 --> 00:12:45.380
they be selling you cigarettes.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:49.760
No joke! Or watching a game show and then
being sold dishwasher detergent in the

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:50.120
middle.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:53.450
One piece of evidence that was used
against ads was that in the US, the

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:57.860
coverage of Queen Elizabeth's coronation
was interrupted by a commercial with a

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:03.410
dancing monkey named J Fred Muggs. The
British considered advertisements like

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:06.529
that embarrassing and quite frankly rude.

00:13:06.529 --> 00:13:12.230
You don't interrupt their queen with a
monkey! So 1954 and 1955 were years that

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:17.510
were filled with debates about when and
how to allow ads onto TV and if the

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.130
behavior of Becky and Joe--

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:23.540
'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared' creators--is any
indication, the issue of ads influencing

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.760
TV is a big concern of theirs.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:29.300
According to the newspaper "The Guardian,"
Becky and Joe received offers from more

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:32.060
mainstream sources to make episodes of
'Don't Hug Me,'

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:35.480
but turned them all down and funded the
project themselves through Kickstarter.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.320
Joe Pelling was quoted as saying, "We
wanted to keep it fairly odd and have

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:41.810
the freedom to keep doing exactly what
we wanted."

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:46.040
The implication here is clear! If 'Don't
Hug Me, I'm Scared' were made for

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:49.430
television, it would have had to have
been approved by other people, people

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:53.839
whose interests would have been for
profit or popularity, not about the

00:13:53.839 --> 00:13:57.680
message of the show. And when you look at
these 6 episodes it's pretty clear that

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:02.270
the messages for a positive, good,
inspirational, entertaining kids show are

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:06.800
there, but they've been perverted by the
influence of an outside force.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.600
'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared' is ultimately a
parable for the loss of control that

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:14.649
artists trade off when they work on
bigger screens.

00:14:14.649 --> 00:14:18.819
Their messages are manipulated and their
morals are poisoned by others with

00:14:18.819 --> 00:14:20.259
ulterior motives.

00:14:20.259 --> 00:14:24.579
And with that, I'm going to have to leave
you hanging. I know! I know! I'm sorry! I

00:14:24.579 --> 00:14:28.089
recognize that we're just starting to
scratch the surface of this series, but

00:14:28.089 --> 00:14:32.139
establishing this series as a launching
point was really important for answering

00:14:32.139 --> 00:14:35.619
all the other questions that we haven't
gotten a chance to touch on today.

00:14:35.619 --> 00:14:40.899
Who are these characters, really? What's
the significance of June 19? What about Roy,

00:14:40.899 --> 00:14:45.939
the ominous lurking figure The Yellow
Guy claims is his dad? And what does the

00:14:45.939 --> 00:14:50.019
ending mean? But don't worry we're getting
to all those questions and more in just

00:14:50.019 --> 00:14:56.829
another week! Which means that the lesson
of today is subscribing! Subscribing is a

00:14:56.829 --> 00:15:04.569
good idea if you want to see the videos
when they come out!"

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:12.640
Subscribe! And IN THE MEANTIME, REMEMBER:
"That's just a theory! A FILM THEORY!"

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:14.800
and

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:15.800
CUT!

